designs.costs:
Contract:
- entry points 137 -> 151 (audio) -> 161 (video);
- 8 signature moves, each with a Q-ruling;
- 2 wires (21/25 used);
- about 37 new levers, each needing a census amendment row, a LEVERS READ line and a docs/05 re-render;
- 2 new packages registered in 5 places;
- about 12 new compose rows plus join edits (_signature_width, _alphabet_size, _base_parameters, _geometry_manifest, _signature_stream, _sample_window, _head, _periods, _bytes_per_window);
- rulings on M1, the ID layout, MEM positions and absent checkpoint fields.

Compute per window: unchanged. Windows stay 128 ids, and the measured step cost is identical for text and audio windows. The added cost is windows per media second:
- 0.2 windows per audio-second at 25 Hz, 0.39 at 50 Hz;
- 32 tokens/s video at the default = 0.25 windows per video-second;
- paired examples multiply caption length 3.8-6.4x.
Under the 12-runs-per-card slowdown, 1 h of audio costs 4-12 min of wall time per run late in training.

Codec phase: a 0.65M-param codec step (batch 8 x 0.5 s) cost about 250 ms on 1 CPU thread; small on GPU. It runs only for AUD_TRAIN_WINDOWS.

Memory: media rows 1002 x 128 x 2 (emb + head) = 0.26M params per modality at width 128.

Data build: offline encoding at 66-90x real time on 1 CPU thread, re-run once per freeze.

Rows in MEM, DOM tokc and SIG alphabet grow by the media rows (about +25% at 4096 slots).

judge.winner:
Route 1 (one shared discrete token stream, early fusion) wins, with large grafts from Route 3 (Hybrid) and targeted grafts from Route 2 (WORLD-continuous).

Routes 1 and 3 are close: they share the same core of learned FSQ codes, per-modality LM tables outside the BPE id space, a modality-masked softmax, a codec that is frozen after its own phase, and text rehearsal. Route 1 wins on two counts:
- It is the only design measured inside the tree's own LM and WORLD. That includes forgetting with WORLD in the loop, and the 25 Hz versus 50 Hz LM comparison.
- It makes DOM, FAB and MEM do modality-aware work, which is goal B.

The merged design takes from Route 3:
- the WORLD temporal media head over the frozen codec's continuous latents;
- the spectral codec as the default codec architecture;
- the transport-id guards;
- fresh synthetic clips every epoch;
- the quiet-decoder gauge;
- its minimal-contract tactics (root-side filtering instead of signature moves wherever filtering costs no function).

It takes from Route 2:
- the continuous insert path for understanding, as the first S8 arm;
- the free-running rollout loss for any WORLD-rollout generation arm;
- the reporting rule that one-step WORLD scores are not generation quality.

issues.evidence:
K12 (tests/test_contract.py:3019-3094) requires a deferral reason to name every required ARGUMENT that has no producer. The plan says the reasons 'name AUD.loss_terms and EVAL.grounding', which are consumers. DATA.recover's `wave` and DATA.media_batch's `n`/`crop_s` would go unnamed and fail.

K13 checks per-package lever counts in section headings (fixture 'levers:DATA'). docs/04_CONTRACT.md:482 reads 'DATA ... (18 levers)'; S1 adds 9. Other counts that move:
- '75 levers carry U.FRACTION' (:281) moves with media_holdout and pair_caption_first;
- OPT's '(one of them a census amendment — there are two in the tree)' (:581) moves with 9+ amendment rows;
- the levers total moves.
The plan mentions only the entry-point count (137 -> 139).

OPT_BATCH_WINDOWS defaults to 1 (opt/levers.py:841), and gpu_world.sh does not change it. So 'AUD.loss_terms once per flush' means one codec step (batch 16 x 0.5 s, STFT plus convs) PER WINDOW. That is 45-60 ms on CPU against about 275 ms per window (3.64 w/s): +16-22%. On GPU, 38 w/s early is about 26 ms per window, and a small-kernel codec step under 12-way MPS sharing is unmeasured ('small on GPU' is an assertion).

Other per-window or per-flush costs outside the '34-39 ms unchanged' claim:
- WORLD.media_terms runs a sequential GRU over F frames per segment each flush;
- mixed windows need a per-position (B, L, V+1002) modality mask, which LM.decode's current (live_vocab, n_retired) mask cache (lm/api.py:802-818) cannot cache;
- EVAL.grounding.
With an unexplained ~15x late slowdown under investigation, none of these should enter a default without a GPU number.

critic.missing:
1. A timeline for the owner's actual run shape (RUN_EPOCHS=1, one ~20 MB stream, 20k windows, batch 1). The design never places the codec phase, the freeze, the media phases and the media share on that timeline. Every mechanism it hangs on the epoch roll needs an in-epoch equivalent: encode/interleave, fresh clips, the freeze re-encode.

2. A codec-independent stream layout (fixed-length placeholders), so that the geometry measured at compose stays true after the freeze: run_windows, windows_in_epoch, the OPT horizon.

3. An audit of every per-id tensor op, with ids >= 2^24 routed through one row-mapping join, plus a guard test.

4. A per-modality split of the per-flush loss curve the owner's GPU analysis uses.

5. An owner, a save path and a geometry rule for the per-modality competence baselines.

6. A GPU cost measurement of the codec step, the WORLD media GRU and one grounding pass under 12-way MPS, before any default is fixed.

7. A stored d97779d baseline fixture, captured before S1 edits, to make 'bit-identical' checkable.

8. Measured analytic inverses for vowels and melody. Only tones is verified: 360/360, re-run by me.

9. What the codec phase does to OPT's LR schedule. The LM's cosine horizon is run_windows, so media phases arriving after a 20k-window codec phase would start at a decayed LR, and no re-warm is specified for the first media phase. OPT_LR_SHIFT_WARM appears only among the isolation arms.

10. A seed plan: every quoted result is one seed. The owner's own D-A13 note says seeds must vary the synthetic data, so the synthetic clips must be keyed on RUN.seed too.

